speechtext:
Mr. Chairman, I listened with a great deal of interest to the remarks made, a few days ago, by the hon. member for L'Islet and the hon. minister of the Interior, concerning immigration, as well as to the remarks made to-day by hon. gentlemen opposite in reference to the same matter.
That question of immigration is of vital importance, from the standpoint of our country's future, and it is time the Government should step in and provide some wise enactment with a view to shutting out that class of immigrants that is swarming into our cities, those Jews who are a curse to the trade; those ruffians and thugs who terrorize our peaceful citizens; those criminals whom we are constrained as a last resort, to confine to our penitentiaries, after costly lawsuits, at the expense of honest people.
Last year, I noticed a resolution passed by the Montreal Chambre de Commerce concerning immigration, which appears to me to be very much to the point. It seems to

me the Government should not overlook the matter. It reads as follows:
The Council of the Chambre de Commerce
of the district of Montreal respectfully submits :
1. That, within the last few years, immigration to Canada has been increasing rapidly;
2. That Canada is especially interested in admitting such immigrants only who are worthy of sharing with its inhabitants all the rights and privileges granted by its constitution ;
3.	That Canadian business men are particularly concerned with the business reliability and solvency of such immigrants;
4.	That under the present system of immigration and naturalization, foreigners are admitted into the country under an assumed name, which they are free to change one or several times, when starting some new business;
5.	That business men have no means of protecting themselves against such abusive substitution of names, which oftentimes is the occasion of huge frauds detrimental to the trade;
6.	That often the rights of citizenship are conferred on foreigners without previously making enquiries as to compliance, or otherwise, with the requirements of the law;
7.	That foreigners are granted the rights of British citizenship, though they have not resided three years in Canada as required;
Wherefore the council of the ' Chambre de Commerce du district de Montreal ' humbly requests the Right Hon. R. L. Borden, Prime Minister of Canada, and his hon. colleagues, to so amend the immigration and naturalization acts as to require:
(a.) That every immigrant show on entering Canada, his identification card and his passport, signed by the authorities of the place whence he comes, and bearing his name, surname, age, profession, nationality, the name of the sailing port and of the company which brought him here, together with a description of the bearer of the card ;
(b.) That the said card and passport should be taken down verbatim in the books of the immigration officials, on his admittance into Canada, and that there and then an indentifl-cation card be handed to the said foreigner by the Canadian authorities; the said card to be the exact copy of that entered in the book of the immigration office;
(c.) That the said foreigner wishing to do business in Canada should again show the said card on registering the name of his business firm, thus giving his actual name, together with the firm's name; that the same card should be available in connection with real estate transactions ;
(d.) That in order to be granted naturalization, every foreigner should exhibit his identification card showing that he has resided in the country for a period of three years;
(e.) That his application for naturalization should be made personally, and that he should moreover show certificates from two British subjects certifying to the good reputation of the applicant;
(f.) That a heavy penalty should be imposed for any violation of the law in this respect.
And you will do justice.
La Chambre de Commerce du district de Montreal.
All of which is respectfully submitted.

That resolution was surely very well taken, and should the Government act on it, some serious mistakes which are very hurtful to our trade would be avoided.
In connection with that immigration question, I may say that in visiting of late the penitentiary of St. Vincent de Paul, I found on going over the long list of inmates that 50 per cent of the names entered in the book are those of people hailing from Montreal, or who had been convicted there. How explain that Montreal, which represents only one quarter of the whole population of the province of Quebec, is responsible for such a high proportion of those criminals? Montreal's everyday people are surely as honest and peaceful as those inhabiting the country districts or the other cities of the province. Therefore, I say, if Montreal is responsible for a larger proportion of criminals, that is due evidently to outside causes, to causes which might, it seems to me, be kept under control to a certain extent. The primary cause is the large number of undesirables who come in by the hundreds of thousands every year, a goodly proportion of which select Montreal as their settling point, I refer particularly to workingmen. If the high rate of crimin-alty in Montreal is due to the influx of foreign labour at that point, would it not be wise to impede, or even to bring to a complete standstill that inflow of immigrants? Such a state of things is highly detrimental, in the first place, to our own workingmen who very often have in the winter months to suffer through unemployment, and, besides, the number of criminals is being increased in alarming proportions.
The general [DOT] feeling throughout the country is, I believe, that these aliens should be entirely excluded from this country, and that we should grant admittance to those only who come with their families to reside there. Thus we would be sure to get a better and steadier class of immigrants.
We have trade commissioners in Great Britain whom we pay most liberally; why should they not act as immigration inspectors at the sailing ports, previous to the departure of the ships that carry these immigrants?
The hon. Minister of the Interior (Mr. Roche), judging from his recent utterances, seems to be quite satisfied with the numbers of immigrants reaching our shores from year to year. As for me, I find we are getting them in too large numbers; their numbers should be reduced materially, and quality be made to make up for the reduction in quantity. Our legislation should 103
be more exacting, and the regulations carried out in every respect as regards aliens coming from those countries whence thugs and tramps are despatched. It should at the same time provide for the exclusion of Jews, as well as of Chinamen and Hindus. Every immigrant should be required to show, previous to being admitted into Canada, a certificate from the municipal authorities of his country, and be the possessor of several hundred dollars at least in cash, whenever he seeks admittance in any other capacity than that of agricultural settler; and lastly, none should be admitted unless they come with their families and with the intention of settling on farms.
In that way we would avoid being overrun by the rabble of European countries, and we would protect Canadian labour, which at times is out of employment, while those aliens are at work, pocketing our cash with a view to sending it over to their own country.
I shall now deal with the immigration originating in the republic to the south of us. There exists a certain immigration made up of our own people who are returning from the United States. That movement is kept up for the most part by repatriation agents appointed by us to work in that country and who take their instructions from the Department of the Interior.
After looking into certain reports and witnessing certain facts, I am under the impression that such agents are not doing the work which they were appointed to do, and that the country does not profit by their services in proportion to the expenditure entailed. I do not say that such agencies should not exist, but I attribute their failure to inefficiency on the part of several of the officers appointed to fill those positions, and who are not possessed of the knowledge and qualifications required to properly carry on the work. A clipping from an editorial which I hold in my hand shows that I am not alone in being struck by such inefficiency. It reads as follows:
Why did the Dominion government appoint an inveterate ' monolinguist ' to discourage the repatriation of French Canadians settled in the United States? Does the immigration policy of the Government consist in excluding Frenchspeaking people? How is it that French Canadians in Vermont, Connecticut, Massachusetts and Maine are unable to make themselves understood by the man whom the Government has appointed to look after their interests? Why has not a man speaking at least two languages been appointed to that position?
And further on, in the same editorial:
It is high time that Mr. Baulby should be removed, if he is to continue discouraging people.



while the French Canadian agent is ' left ' to exert himself in a state where he has not much chance of success.
Mr. Baulby is immigration agent at Boston; he was appointed previous to the coming into power of this Government, but I understand that conditions have not changed in the meantime. Boston is a very large centre where numbers of French Canadians go by. It seems to me there should be at that place an agent understanding both languages.
